    "type": "question",
    "label": "12",
    "prompt": "Draw a diagram and prove that the diagonals of a parallelogram bisect each other.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$AX=CX$ and $BX=DX$.",
      "worked": "Let diagonals AC and BD intersect at X. The opposite sides satisfy $AD=CB$. Since $AD\\parallel CB$, $\\angle DAX=\\angle BCX$ (alternate angles). Also $\\angle DXA=\\angle BXC$ (vertically opposite). Thus $\\triangle DAX\\cong\\triangle BCX$ by AAS. Corresponding sides give $AX=CX$ and $DX=BX$, so the diagonals bisect each other.",
